==English==

===Etymology===
Recorded since 1647, from {{der|en|ML.|culminatus}}, the past participle of {{m|la|culminare||to crown}}, from {{der|en|la|culmen||peak, the highest point}}, older form {{m|la|columen||top, summit}}, from a {{der|en|ine-pro|-}} base {{m|ine-pro|*kelH-|*kol-, *kel-|to project, rise; peak, summit, top}}, whence also {{m+|en|hill}} and {{m|en|holm}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkʌlmɪneɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-uk-culminate.ogg|Audio (UK)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|culminat}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive|astronomy}} Of a [[heavenly body]], to be at the highest point, reach its greatest altitude.
# {{lb|en|intransitive|also|figuratively}} To [[reach]] the (physical) [[summit]], highest point, [[peak]] etc.
#: {{syn|en|peak}}
#* {{RQ:Milton PL|3|passage=As when his beams at noon / '''Culminate''' from the equator.}}
#* {{rfdatek|en|Dana}}
#*: The reptile race '''culminated''' in the secondary era.
#* {{RQ:Motley Rise}}
#*: The house of Burgundy was rapidly '''culminating'''.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2019|month=October|author=Tony Miles and Philip Sherratt|title=EMR kicks off new era|journal=Modern Railways|page=53|text=This '''culminates''' in a timetable change in December 2020, at which point EMR will introduce a sixth train each hour out of St Pancras.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|figuratively}} To reach a [[climax]]; to come to the decisive point (especially as an end or conclusion).
#* {{quote-web|en|date=2006-09-12|title=President Bush’s Reality| work=New York Times| url=http://www.nytimes.com/2006/09/12/opinion/12tue1.html| passage=Mr. Bush has been marking the fifth anniversary of Sept. 11 with a series of speeches about terrorism that '''culminated''' with his televised address last night.}}
#: {{ux|en|Their messy breakup '''culminated''' in a restraining order.}}
#: {{ux|en|The class will '''culminate''' with a rigorous examination.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[finalize]], bring to a [[conclusion]], form the climax of.
#* '''2010''', "By the skin of her teeth", ''The Economist'', 7 Sep 2010:
#*: The announcement by Tony Windsor and Rob Oakeshott in Canberra '''culminated''' more than a fortnight of intensive political horse-trading.

